    text encoding
    table Character Meaning
      'r' | open for reading (default)
      'w' | open for writing, truncating the file first
      'x' | open for exclusive creation, failing if the file already exists
      'a' | open for writing, appending to the end of file if it exists
      'b' | binary mode
      't' | text mode (default)
      '+' | open for updating (reading and writing)
    text The default mode is 'r' (open for reading text, a synonym of 'rt'). Modes 'w+' and 'w+b' open and truncate the file. Modes 'r+' and 'r+b' open the file with no truncation.
    text As mentioned in the , Python distinguishes between binary and text I/O. Files opened in binary mode (including  in the  argument) return contents as  objects without any decoding. In text mode (the default, or when  is included in the  argument), the contents of the file are returned as , the bytes having been first decoded using the default encoding or using the specified  if given.
