    text The following example uses the  parameter of the  function to open a file relative to a given directory:
    row
      link dir_fd ../library/os.html#dir-fd
      link os.open() ../library/os.html#os.open
    text >>> import os >>> dir_fd = os.open('somedir', os.O_RDONLY) >>> def opener(path, flags): ... return os.open(path, flags, dir_fd=dir_fd) ... >>> with open('spamspam.txt', 'w', opener=opener) as f: ... print('This will be written to somedir/spamspam.txt', file=f) ... >>> os.close(dir_fd) # don't leak a file descriptor
    text The type of  returned by the  function depends on the mode. When  is used to open a file in a text mode (, , , , etc.), it returns a subclass of  (specifically ). When used to open a file in a binary mode with buffering, the returned class is a subclass of . The exact class varies: in read binary mode, it returns an ; in write binary and append binary modes, it returns an , and in read/write mode, it returns an . When buffering is disabled, the raw stream, a subclass of , , is returned.
