      text Note
      text This is an advanced function that is not needed in everyday Python programming, unlike .
      link importlib.import_module() ../library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module
    text This function is invoked by the  statement. It can be replaced (by importing the  module and assigning to ) in order to change semantics of the  statement, but doing so is  discouraged as it is usually simpler to use import hooks (see ) to attain the same goals and does not cause issues with code which assumes the default import implementation is in use. Direct use of  is also discouraged in favor of .
    link import ../reference/simple_stmts.html#import
    link builtins ../library/builtins.html#module-builtins
    text builtins.__import__
    text import
    text strongly
    link "PEP 302" https://peps.python.org/pep-0302/
    text __import__()
    link importlib.import_module() ../library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module
    text The function imports the module , potentially using the given  and  to determine how to interpret the name in a package context. The  gives the names of objects or submodules that should be imported from the module given by . The standard implementation does not use its  argument at all and uses its  only to determine the package context of the  statement.
    text name
    text globals
    text locals
    text fromlist
    text name
    text locals
    text globals
    link import ../reference/simple_stmts.html#import
    text specifies whether to use absolute or relative imports.  (the default) means only perform absolute imports. Positive values for  indicate the number of parent directories to search relative to the directory of the module calling  (see  for the details).
    text level
    text 0
    text level
    text __import__()
    link "PEP 328" https://peps.python.org/pep-0328/
    text When the name variable is of the form package.module, normally, the top-level package (the name up till the first dot) is returned, not the module named by name. However, when a non-empty fromlist argument is given, the module named by name is returned.
    text For example, the statement import spam results in bytecode resembling the following code:
    text spam = __import__('spam', globals(), locals(), [], 0)
    text The statement import spam.ham results in this call:
    text spam = __import__('spam.ham', globals(), locals(), [], 0)
    text Note how  returns the toplevel module here because this is the object that is bound to a name by the  statement.
    text __import__()
    link import ../reference/simple_stmts.html#import
    text On the other hand, the statement from spam.ham import eggs, sausage as saus results in
    text _temp = __import__('spam.ham', globals(), locals(), ['eggs', 'sausage'], 0) eggs = _temp.eggs saus = _temp.sausage
    text Here, the spam.ham module is returned from __import__(). From this object, the names to import are retrieved and assigned to their respective names.
    text If you simply want to import a module (potentially within a package) by name, use .
    link importlib.import_module() ../library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module
    text Changed in version 3.3: Negative values for level are no longer supported (which also changes the default value to 0).
    text When the command line options  or  are being used, the environment variable  is now ignored.
    text Changed in version 3.9: danger
